<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patriarch Nikon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a>–1681</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Nikon of Moscow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikon was Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1652 to 1666. A close adviser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexis, he reformed Russian liturgical books and rituals to bring them into line with contemporary Greek practice, ordering for example the sign of the cross with three fingers rather than two. Opposition to the reforms produced the schism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a>, and his claims for church authority against the tsar led to his deposition by a church council in 1666.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patriarch-nikon.jpg" alt="Patriarch Nikon" width="320" height="625" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bezmin, Ivan Artemyev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikon_by_Ivan_Bezmin_(1680s,_Kremlin_museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
